WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.940
What would a synapse need, to store an association?

2
00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:10.310
In 1949, Donald Hebb proposed a rule.

3
00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:15.130
When one cell repeatedly helps fire another, the connection between them grows.

4
00:00:16.233 --> 00:00:18.373
Treat that as a design specification.

5
00:00:19.433 --> 00:00:21.973
The synapse should strengthen when two things happen together.

6
00:00:23.017 --> 00:00:24.717
Its own input is active.

7
00:00:25.357 --> 00:00:27.957
And the cell it talks to is active at the same moment.

8
00:00:28.917 --> 00:00:32.117
First, we need evidence that synapses change at all.

9
00:00:32.857 --> 00:00:35.737
Most of it comes from the hippocampus, so here is a map.

10
00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:39.170
Input arrives from the entorhinal cortex.

11
00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:44.670
Its axons, the perforant path, reach the dentate gyrus and its granule cells.

12
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:51.233
The granule cells' axons, the mossy fibres, reach the pyramidal cells of CA3.

13
00:00:52.267 --> 00:00:57.087
Their axons, the Schaffer collaterals, reach the pyramidal cells of CA1.

14
00:00:58.007 --> 00:01:00.167
Most of this story happens at that last relay.

15
00:01:01.217 --> 00:01:03.397
We will visit three synapses on this chain.

16
00:01:04.257 --> 00:01:06.757
The first is where lasting change was first measured.

17
00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:13.367
In 1973, Tim Bliss and Terje Lømo reported it, in anaesthetized rabbits.

18
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:19.060
They stimulated the perforant path and recorded from the dentate gyrus, with an

19
00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:20.540
electrode outside the cells.

20
00:01:21.583 --> 00:01:26.903
That electrode records a field potential: an extracellular voltage made by the

21
00:01:26.903 --> 00:01:28.463
summed currents of many neurons.

22
00:01:29.533 --> 00:01:31.253
A test pulse gives two parts.

23
00:01:32.153 --> 00:01:36.113
A slow wave, the population EPSP, measures synaptic strength.

24
00:01:37.117 --> 00:01:41.537
A sharp spike riding on it shows how many cells fire, and how closely in time.

25
00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:47.150
Between test pulses, they gave brief high-frequency trains, called a tetanus.

26
00:01:47.990 --> 00:01:51.990
One regimen was 100 pulses per second, for 3 to 4 seconds.

27
00:01:52.983 --> 00:01:58.143
Afterwards, the same test pulse gave a larger response, in 15 of 18 rabbits.

28
00:01:59.103 --> 00:02:01.183
It lasted from 30 minutes to 10 hours.

29
00:02:02.217 --> 00:02:05.857
This lasting rise is long-term potentiation, or LTP.

30
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.380
How do you know the change belongs to that input?

31
00:02:10.540 --> 00:02:13.660
Record a second pathway to the same cells, and leave it untrained.

32
00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:17.950
If the preparation were drifting, that pathway would drift too.

33
00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:22.790
If it stays flat while the trained one rises, the change is input specific.

34
00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:28.240
In a made-up recording, the slope rose to 160% % of baseline.

35
00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:31.660
The spike rose to 250%.

36
00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:34.040
Is the synapse two and a half times stronger?

37
00:02:40.133 --> 00:02:44.993
No. Synaptic strength is read from the slope, and it rose much less.

38
00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:47.513
Firing has a threshold.

39
00:02:48.373 --> 00:02:52.473
A modest rise in the EPSP pushes many more cells past it.

40
00:02:53.053 --> 00:02:54.573
So the spike grows out of proportion.

41
00:02:55.717 --> 00:03:01.317
To test whether the cells became more excitable, compare spike size at matched EPSP

42
00:03:01.317 --> 00:03:02.317
size.

43
00:03:02.617 --> 00:03:04.317
The record leaves three puzzles.

44
00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:08.317
How can a synapse detect that both cells were active together?

45
00:03:09.137 --> 00:03:11.017
What decides whether it goes up or down?

46
00:03:11.897 --> 00:03:13.157
And does memory use it?

47
00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.660
One receptor answers the first puzzle, and offers a model for the second.

48
00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:27.900
At Schaffer collateral–CA1 synapses, with normal magnesium outside the cell, LTP is

49
00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:31.300
triggered by calcium entering through NMDA receptors.

50
00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:36.820
Substantial entry needs glutamate and enough local depolarization together.
